<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Ferrucci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Francesco Ferruccio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Ferrucci (1489–1530) was a Florentine military commander during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1529–1530, when the last republic resisted the imperial and papal armies restoring the Medici. Leading a relief army from Volterra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, he was defeated and captured at Gavinana on 3 August 1530 and killed by the imperial captain Fabrizio Maramaldo. He later became a hero of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-ferrucci.jpg" alt="Francesco Ferrucci" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uffizi_16,_Francesco_Ferrucci.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferrucci worked for a few years as a merchant's clerk before taking up soldiering at an early age. He served his apprenticeship under Giovanni de' Medici in the Black Bands, named after the black stripes on Giovanni's insignia, in various parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and earned a reputation as a daring fighter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> moved to reinstate the Medici in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac">War of the League of Cognac</a>, Ferrucci was appointed Florentine military commissioner and harried the Imperials with rapid marches and sudden attacks. Early in 1530 he surprised and recaptured Volterra, which had thrown off Florentine allegiance and taken in an Imperial garrison, but in his absence the Imperials took Empoli by treachery, cutting one of the chief routes to Florence.</p><p>Named commissioner-general for the operations outside the city, he proposed marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to frighten the pope into a favourable peace, but the war committee rejected the plan as too audacious. He resolved instead to strike the Imperials in the rear from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, but a month's fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> let the enemy learn of his plan. At the end of July he left Pisa with about 4,000 men; a sortie from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> to support him was prevented by the commander in the city, Malatesta Baglioni.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At Gavinana his attack at first drove the Imperials back, and their commander, Philibert of Châlon, Prince of Orange, was killed, but the arrival of 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknecht</a> under Fabrizio Maramaldo almost annihilated the Florentines. According to popular accounts, Ferrucci's last words to Maramaldo were &quot;Vile, tu uccidi un uomo morto!&quot; (Coward, you kill a dead man!). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> surrendered nine days later, and the Italian verb maramaldeggiare came to mean to bully a defenceless victim.</p><p>During the Risorgimento Ferrucci became a metaphor for the struggles of the day. Francesco Domenico Guerrazzi's novel L'Assedio di Firenze glorified his life, Goffredo Mameli named him in Il Canto degli Italiani of 1847, and in an 1849 speech at Livorno Garibaldi declared that he would know how to die like Ferruccio. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> a festival in his name was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, which partly accounts for the popularity of the name Ferruccio among Tuscan boys born then.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco Ferruccio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last republic defended itself during the siege of 1529–1530, with fortifications designed by Michelangelo, until <b>Francesco Ferrucci</b>’s relief army was destroyed at Gavinana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in San Marcello Pistoiese, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesco Ferrucci"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
